The example movie for which I defined the frames in the question above can now be exported as follows:

exportMov["slowDownMovie.mov", frames,
Append[Range[Length[frames] - 1]/20, 2]]

The frames are first exported as single images into a temporary directory, then assembled into a movie with the name given as the first argument.

The third argument to exportMov
is the list of frame durations, and that is of course the whole point of this function. In the above example, I used the same list of durations as for the GIF
animation. The list of durations is optional (when omitted, a constant default duration is used). If the list of durations is shorter than the list of frames, then the given durations are repeated cyclically until all frames are used up.

